<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luchuan–Pingmian campaigns</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1436">1436</a>–<a href="/years/1449">1449</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Luchuan–Pingmian campaigns were a series of Ming Chinese military expeditions from 1436 to 1449 against the Shan state of Mong Mao, called Luchuan by the Chinese, on the south-western frontier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>. They were directed against the ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/si-renfa">Si Renfa</a>, who had expanded his power at the expense of other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>. After costly fighting he fled to Ava, which eventually handed him over, and Mong Mao was broken up.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luchuan-pingmian-campaigns.jpg" alt="Luchuan–Pingmian campaigns" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 瑞丽江的河水 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%BA%93%E5%B7%9D%E4%B9%8B%E5%BD%B9-%E6%80%BB%E5%9B%BE-%E8%8B%B1%E6%96%87%E7%89%88.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Möng Mao, which the Ming called Luchuan–Pingmian, was a tusi or pacification commission on the upper Shweli River, roughly the modern districts of Longchuan and Ruili. Si Renfa succeeded his brother Si Hangfa in 1413. In November 1437 he annexed the lands of the tusi of Nandian, and the regional commander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, Mu Sheng, reported in 1438 that Möng Mao had repeatedly invaded its neighbours and appointed chieftains without the court's approval. The emperor ordered a punitive campaign on 8 December 1438.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In the first campaign Fang Zheng crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salween">Salween</a> at night against Mu Sheng's wishes and drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/si-renfa">Si Renfa</a> back, but with exhausted troops and cut supply lines he fell into an ambush of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> at Kongni and died with his men. Mu Sheng withdrew and died of illness on 2 May 1439.</p><p>Over the objections of officials such as He Wenyuan, and with the backing of the eunuch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-zhen-eunuch">Wang Zhen</a>, who had the critic Liu Qiu executed, a second campaign began on 27 February 1441. After several battles the Ming stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/si-renfa">Si Renfa</a>'s stronghold at Shangjiang, where his side reportedly lost 50,000 men, and took his base in January 1442. Si Renfa fled to Ava, whose king held him for ransom.</p><p>His son Si Jifa fought on. A third expedition under Wang Ji in 1443 took his base and family, but he escaped to Mong Yang. In April 1445 Ava agreed to surrender <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/si-renfa">Si Renfa</a> in return for Ming support in annexing part of Hsenwi; he was executed in January 1446. A last campaign of 1449 stormed Si Jifa's stockades, but he escaped again, and accounts of his end disagree.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Si Lunfa's family survived as rulers of Mong Yang under Ming watch, on condition that they never return to Möng Mao, and their successors invaded Ava in 1527. The historian Wang Gongwu describes the war as disastrous for the Ming: it drained the south-western provinces, cost the respect of border allies and drew troops from the north just before the defeat in which the emperor was captured. The campaigns and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumu-crisis">Tumu Crisis</a> that followed are seen as the turning point into Ming decline.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Luchuan–Pingmian campaigns” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Si Renfa defied the Ming, who launched the <b>Luchuan-Pingmian campaigns</b> of 1436 to 1449; he fled to Ava, which handed him over.</blockquote><a href="/state/mong-mao">Mong Mao</a></li></ul></section>
